{0 Abort and timeouts}

{!Workero.Abort_controller} and {!Workero.Abort_signal} wrap [AbortController]
and [AbortSignal]. A signal tells running work to stop. Use one to put a
timeout on a request or to stop a streaming loop when the client leaves.

{1 Time out a request}

Pass a signal to {!Workero.fetch} or {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch}.
{!Workero.Abort_signal.timeout} aborts after a number of milliseconds:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match fetch ~signal:(Abort_signal.timeout 2000) "https://example.com/slow" with
  | res -> Response.text (Response.body_text res)
  | exception (Js_error _ as exn) when Abort_signal.is_abort exn ->
      Response.text ~status:`Gateway_Timeout "upstream too slow\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

When the signal aborts, the runtime rejects the request. The call raises
{!Workero.Js_error}. The reason is a [TimeoutError] for a timeout and an
[AbortError] otherwise; {!Workero.Abort_signal.is_abort} is [true] for both. The
signal also cancels the reading of the response body: {!Workero.Response.body_text}
raises the same way. A signal that has already aborted fails the call without
sending a request.

{1 Abort by hand}

{!Workero.Abort_controller.create} makes a controller, and
{!Workero.Abort_controller.signal} gives its signal. {!Workero.Abort_controller.abort}
aborts it, with an optional message that becomes the message of the
[AbortError]. {!Workero.Abort_signal.any} combines signals: it aborts with the
first one.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let stop = Abort_controller.create () in
  let signal =
    Abort_signal.any [ Abort_controller.signal stop; Abort_signal.timeout 2000 ]
  in
  ignore (Timer.set_timeout (fun () -> Abort_controller.abort stop) 500);
  match fetch ~signal "https://example.com/slow" with
  | _ -> Response.text "finished\n"
  | exception (Js_error _ as exn) when Abort_signal.is_abort exn ->
      Response.text "canceled\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{1 What an abort cancels}

An abort cancels the I/O, not the fiber. The fiber wakes up with an exception
when the I/O that it awaits is rejected. Workero cannot stop a fiber that
computes or that awaits something else. Two tools help:

- {!Workero.Abort_signal.aborted} and {!Workero.Abort_signal.throw_if_aborted}
  tell a loop to stop between steps.
- {!Workero.Abort_signal.to_promise} rejects when the signal aborts. Race it
  against any other promise to stop waiting:
  [Promise.await (Promise.race [p; Abort_signal.to_promise signal])]. The work
  behind [p] keeps running; {!Workero.Timer.sleep} cannot be canceled.

{!Workero.Abort_signal.on_abort} runs a function once, in its own fiber, when
the signal aborts. The function may call {!Workero.Promise.await}. An exception
that it raises goes to [console.error].

{1 Client cancellation}

{!Workero.Request.signal} is the signal of the incoming request. It aborts when
the client cancels the request. It needs the [enable_request_signal]
compatibility flag in [wrangler.toml]: [compatibility_flags = ["enable_request_signal"]].
The project template sets it. Without the flag, the signal never aborts.

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let gone = ref false in
  Abort_signal.on_abort (Request.signal req) (fun () -> gone := true);
  Response.stream ctx (fun w ->
      while not !gone do
        Stream.Writer.write w "tick\n";
        ignore (Promise.await (Timer.sleep 1000))
      done)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Do not pass [Request.signal] to work that must outlive the client, such as a
{!Workero.fetch} inside {!Workero.Context.wait_until}.

{2 What was verified}

In [workerd] 2026-10-19 with [enable_request_signal], aborting a [fetch] to a
Worker that streams its response fires the signal of that Worker's request
while the stream is open. With a handler that only awaits a timer, the earlier
spike saw no abort event in [workerd serve]. Do not rely on [Request.signal]
to detect every client disconnect. Workero has not checked the behavior on
Cloudflare production.

{!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} raises {!Workero.Js_error} after a disconnect,
with a reason that is not an [AbortError], so {!Workero.Abort_signal.is_abort}
is [false] for it. Treat any [Js_error] from a write as a disconnect.

{1 Where signals apply}

[?signal] exists on {!Workero.fetch}, {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch} and
{!Workero.Fetcher.fetch_request}. {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch_request} builds a new
request with the signal and leaves the signal of the request that you pass
unchanged. The new signal replaces the one that the request has; it does not
combine them. Use {!Workero.Abort_signal.any} for that. Bindings such as KV, R2,
D1 and Cache take no signal.
